Z||000015||FieroFanaticus2||03-31-2001||10:07 PM||||Yeah, there's more to life than Fiero's.  But give up everything???  What about T.V dinners and ready made pizza's?  But, then again, I could give that up to see tons of hot chicks walking around in barely nothing all the time...unfortunatly I would have to stay in the house a lot of tape it to my leg then.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000016||TwisT||04-01-2001||01:38 AM||||...I'll be sure to let you guys know how cool it is picking fruit off a tree right outside your door...<P>...And how cool it is catching your own lobster and eating it.<P>To hell with TV dinners!  LOL...<P>Brett||||reg||1
Z||000017||Formula88||04-01-2001||02:32 AM||||Dude, that is so awesome!  Congratulations!  It really sucks not to keep your Fiero, but it may be worth it.  Still, you gotta have a car, right?!?!  Maybe you'll figure out a way to take it with you by Fall.  <P>And, yea, the internet meeting can be dangerous, but let me share a little story about two very close friends of mine:<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/nick&marcy.jpg">  <BR>Marcella and Nick.<P>He's from Scotland, was working in the US.  She's from Italy.  They met online and fell in love.  She flew out here to meet him in person, and it blossomed from there.  To make a long story short, they are now married, living in Italy, and just had a baby girl this Spring!  It's a fairy tale, but sometimes they do come true!!<P>Me, I don't consider myself a hopeless romantic, I'm a hope<B><I>full</B></I> romantic!  Congrats on finding "The One"!  May the two of you live as long as you want, but not want for as long as you live.  <p>[This message has been edited by Formula88 (edited 04-01-2001).]||||reg||14
Z||000018||TwisT||04-01-2001||02:47 AM||||Formula,  thanks for the kind words...   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>I myself would have never guessed that I would be doing this,  and I can guarantee I wouldn't if I wasn't sure of myself.  Kat knows me better than I know myself...  <P>...And the Fiero will be missed!  What especially sucks is that I finally got to the point where my Fiero is ready for all the mods I wanted to do.  Now,  I'm stuck with going through all the work and having to sell it when it's done.  That sucks!<P>I'll get over it tho.<P>Brett||||reg||1
Z||000019||GT Bastard||04-01-2001||11:51 AM||||Well Brett, if you have trouble selling it just leave it at my Grandmas house in Yankton.  I'll pick it up whenever I have time.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000020||Formula88||04-01-2001||12:19 PM||||Or you could keep it in storage stateside for when you come back to visit!  ||||reg||1
Z||000021||TwisT||04-01-2001||01:24 PM||||Jeff,  that's not a bad idea,  but I don't want your grandma' doing burn-outs and cookies with it when you're not lookin'.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>I'd keep the damn thing if I could.  It's not that keeping it for when I came to visit would be a bad idea,  but I HAVE to sell it to rid myself of a bank loan that I have.  I still owe about $4500 on a loan I took out on my '47 Chevy Coupe that burned up (!) a couple of years ago.  No,  insurance DIDN'T pay for it...  it was one long ass battle in court just to get $2500 out of them.  Anyway,  that's a whole different story in itself...<P>I love my Fiero.  I hate to see it go.  <P>Brett<P>||||reg||1
Z||000022||Songman||04-01-2001||03:25 PM||||Hey Brett! Congratulations man!<P>I would be lying if I said I would do anything different! I gave up a very comfortable career to move to Nashville to chase a dream. Not everyone understood it, but they respected my courage for giving it a try. I wish you both all the luck in the world.<P>Make sure and let me know where you are down there! It would be cool to have a scuba guide that I could also talk Fieros with!<P>See ya!<BR><P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/songman_1.gif"> <BR><A HREF="http://members.home.com/tnfieros" TARGET=_blank>Tennessee Fiero Owners</A>||||reg||1
Z||000023||TwisT||04-01-2001||04:04 PM||||Songman,  I'll be sure to let everyone know how it goes.  When things get comfortable down there and I get settled in,  you guys are more than welcome to come down;  just buy the plane ticket,  you already have a place to stay!  =)<P>Of course,  I'm jumping the gun a bit...  Let me get there first...   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>Brett||||reg||1
Z||000024||Tina||04-01-2001||07:03 PM||||Noooooooooo,(desperate cry) you can't leave yet.<BR>I changed my mind on the color.<BR>(yeah,yeah,I know. Must be a thing) <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif"><BR>So if you don't have your suitcases in your hand yet, please check your E-mail.<P>Tina<P>Anyways, you must be one of the luckiest <BR>people around.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><BR> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by TwisT:<BR><B>   I'm Carribean bound!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/carribean1.jpg">  <BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>||||reg||1
Z||000025||hugh||04-01-2001||07:12 PM||||How do you say it?Car--ib--ean,or car-ibean.You've got to be able to say it if you want to live there.<BR><p>[This message has been edited by hugh (edited 04-01-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000026||kevin||04-01-2001||10:55 PM||||Twist, your story is very interesting. A thought or two from my testosterone minded background. One, never assume a beautiful blond with a gorgeous smile, blue eyes, infectious laugh, a good 'rolly-polly',a house to call home, a job, sunshine, and caribean rum and beer equals ever-lasting bliss (though the formula does sound attractive).Keep in mind that us stud muffins do have a tendancy to wander as we get older. Do not lose all your ties with us state-siders. The life you lived so far will call you back in short time. All your friends and community is impossible to extricate from who you are. My guess is you will be back after the initial fun and romance is gone. THe old saying may be poignant in your case, "You can always remove the man from the country, but you can never remove the country from the man. Hold onto the Fiero. Have your grandma buy and store it. Do something! After the first year your sphincter will start to tighten. You will long for the days of late night cruises on a twisty road stereo blasting, engine at full song. Down-load this page, keep it in a safe place. One day you will read it and conclude the wisdom of the thoughts. At the same time, I applaud your courage. It is a gutsy move. Never EVER give up,hold onto your dreams, butat the same time, never EVER destroy your path that gets you from one camp the next. Don't forget the Coppertone.     ||||reg||1
Z||000027||TwisT||04-02-2001||12:22 AM||||Kevin,  not sure what to say to that one!<P>I appreciate the concern and sharing of wisdom,  however.  That's something you find in the Fiero community in bucket-fulls...<P>I will say this:  there is always going to be a "doubting" frame of mind for this sort of thing,  as it is a rare occurance to actually happen.  Me,  Mr. South Dakota boy,  meeting a beautiful blonde from the Carribean,  and then moving there to live happily ever after.  There's way more to it than that though...  WAY more.  To put it all here would be too off-topic,  so I won't.  Instead I will summerize:<P>SOAP BOX "ON:"<P>We live here in the States where we are born into a lifestyle of greed;  we are told from day one that we will all become millionares,  or be huge rock stars,  or become movie stars with all sorts of fame and fortune.  We live day by day in an environment which tells us that to be accepted,  we need to drive certain cars,  work at certain jobs,  live in a certain sized house,  and have multiple bank accounts to which we can buy anything and everything.  <P>We are obsessed with "things;"  material posessions that to us,  dictate who we are as human beings and how important we are to society.  <P>People try their whole lives to fill their need for happiness through money and material posessions.  What they miss out on is the true meaning of life;  health,  love and giving.  <P>Peace man,  peace...   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>I'm going to the Carribean not because I'm starry-eyed and brain-washed over a "bubbly blonde,"  nor am I going just to drink beer and get a tan (although that is sure to happen).  I'm going because this woman has chosen to be there for me UNCONDITIONALLY.  I wish I could convey to you how much we mean to each other...  but again,  WAYYYYYYY off topic!  <P>Anyway...<P>People there don't care if you drive a Fiero or a U-Go.  They don't care if you have a big diamond ring on your finger.  They don't care if the house you live in has three stories and a double-stall garage to keep your expensive toys in.  They don't care how many bank accounts you keep your money in.  <P>I like that!  <P>Tourists never see the real lifestyle of native Islanders because they are so concerned about "putting in there time" so they can get back to "reality."  Most American's can't comprehend that there is a place on Earth which money is merely a tool,  and not life itself.<P>These lessons I have learned and am extremely thankful I won't live my life in an empty black-hole of material posession and social conformity.  I'm thankful that I can lay on my death-bed and realize that my life DID mean something and that I didn't spend it competing to have the coolest car or biggest house or the most money.<P>SOAP BOX "OFF"<P>So there you have it...  my heart poured out to each of you.  With any luck,  it will make some sense and maybe open some eyes.<P>Yes,  I still love Fieros.  But to keep mine and stay in the States would be insanity.  I don't know just yet how great it will be down there but...<P>I'll be sure to let all of you know!!!!!!!  LOL...<P>Brett<P>  <P>||||reg||1
Z||000028||88 Fiero||04-02-2001||11:19 AM||||Hey Twist...I know what you mean....When my marriage was in the rocks...I met this girl and have known her for almost 2 years...I too am planning to relocate to Northern California...(that's where she lives) and be with her...I'm hoping to go in the next couple of months...Good luck!!!||||reg||1
Z||000029||william_thorniii||04-02-2001||11:55 AM||||TwistT,<P>I applaud your decision and philosophy.  In fact, it is not dissimilar from Ayn Rand's.  I have always wanted to move away from the US to a country that does not worship the false gods of materialism... but I am far too weak.  Perhaps we can draw from one anothers lives to fulfill our desires.  Any time that you are longing for high-fidelity stereo and modified Fiero suspensions, you can live vicariously through me in my material possessions and I can do the same through your enjoyment of life and the simple things...Red Stripe and Appleton Rum.<P>Cheers,<P>William T. Thorn, III<BR> -I will definitely look you up for vacation recomendations... ||||reg||14
Z||000030||Tigger||04-02-2001||11:55 AM||||Best of Luck to ya mon!<P>Brent ||||reg||1
Z||000031||TwisT||04-02-2001||12:34 PM||||Gabe,  congrats...  I was wondering how you were coming along.  A lot of people may say you're whipped...  but ever notice it's the same people that don't have a woman??? LOL...  Thanks,  and good luck to you too.<P>William,  I know I will always carry with me what I have become.  I also feel EXTREMELY fortunate to not only understand the importance of life,  but also to have met someone who understands too.  She just so happens to live in the Carribean!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">  <P>Keep in mind,  I would have just as easily decided to move to,  say,  California or Florida or Canada for that matter.  She's the motivation I have.  She's the one that can't be replaced...<P>I will say that if I ever have the chance to,  I will have a Fiero again.  There's so many cool things I wanted to do to my Fiero that no one else has done.  <P>I'm an artist;  the Fiero was my canvas.  What I'm doing now is throwing away all my paint and paint brushes.  You dig???   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>Tig' mon,  best of 'rasta from me to you!  *sip-PuFF**PuFF**<P>Heh he hee!!!<P>Brett  ||||reg||1
Z||000032||GT Bastard||04-02-2001||01:36 PM||||I think you've got the right idea TwisT.  "It's only when we've lost everything that we're free to do anything... The things you own end up owning you."  I stole that from Fight Club, but I'm sure it's been said before.  I spend way too much time thinking about cars(and playing with them), and I've often thought it's keeping me from doing the important things in life(ie, being more social, turning thoughts into words.) Everyday is an ongoing search for meaning in my life.  The other day I was thinking "It would be nice to have a better-paying job so that I could mod my Fiero's faster."  Then I thought, "What a worthless pile of sh!t I am to think that..."  What possible difference will it make in the end.  I picture myself lying in a pool of blood(dying) wondering if my life has been an utter waste of time, or if I've touched people.  I would suppose that I'm striving to find a balance right now, and it's caused me to ward away things that were dearest to me in order to grow stronger.  So anyhow, I commend you in your efforts to make a serious change in your life, and I pity those who will never understand it(not naming anyone specifically.)<P>best of luck,<P>ps- I'd be interested in buying some of your furniture(aka material possesions!)  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||14
Z||000033||onfire||04-02-2001||02:47 PM||||Not much for words here. But goodluck, have fun,and try not to get a sunburn.  ||||reg||1
Z||000034||TwisT||04-02-2001||02:50 PM||||Awesome Jeff...   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>I think it's obvious there are lots of us here who have the same thinking.  Fight Club happens to be one of my favorite flicks...  in fact have been watching many times in the last month or so.  There is another movie that relays the same message even more so...  The Matrix.  This is the movie that was first responsible for opening my eyes up to the world.  Yes,  a MOVIE helped me.  Not ashamed to admit that...<P>Jeff,  it sounds like you and I have/had the same "disease."  I imagine lots of us have "Fiero-itis" and don't really want to talk about it.  <P>Maybe there should be a new topic section in Pennock for the Fiero addicted.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>Brett ||||reg||1
Z||000035||TwisT||04-02-2001||03:14 PM||||"You are not your job.<P>You are not how much money you have in the bank.<P>You are not the car you drive.<P>You are not the contents of your wallet.<P>You're not your f***in' khaki's...<P>You're the all-singing,  all-dancing crap of the world."<P>                     ~Brad Pit<BR>                           Fight Club||||reg||1
